          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Early Years and Surrealist Phase</strong><br>\nSalvador Dalí was born in 1904 in Figueres, Spain, and attended the Real Academia de Bellas Artes de San Fernando. In the 1920s, he moved to Paris and became associated with the Surrealist movement, where he developed his distinctive style characterized by precise craftsmanship and dreamlike images.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The Persistence of Memory</strong><br>\nOne of Dalí's most notable works is \"The Persistence of Memory,\" painted in 1931, which features melting clocks in a desolate landscape. This iconic painting reflects Dalí's interest in the subconscious and the interpretation of dreams, central themes in his work.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Expulsion from the Surrealist Movement</strong><br>\nDalí's increasingly eccentric behavior and divergent political views led to his expulsion from the official Surrealist group. Despite this, he continued to develop his version of Surrealism, infused with his interests in religion, science, and history.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          }
        ],
        "slideBackgroundSelector": {}
      },
      {
        "elements": [
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
