          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Later Years and Themes</strong><br>\nIn his later years, Dalí's work turned to scientific, historical, and religious themes, combining hyper-realistic style with optical illusions, holography, and complex, meticulous detail. His interest in DNA and religious mysticism featured prominently in his later paintings.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Personal Life and Public Image</strong><br>\nDalí was known for his flamboyant mustache and outlandish public appearances, which were as much a part of his legacy as his artwork. His marriage to Gala, his muse and manager, was a significant personal and professional partnership throughout his career.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Death and Posthumous Fame</strong><br>\nSalvador Dalí passed away in 1989, leaving behind a vast oeuvre that includes painting, sculpture, writing, and film. His work remains a significant contribution to Surrealist art and continues to be celebrated for its innovation, whimsy, and boundless creativity.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
